<http://data.silknow.org/object/e3e6223c-62ad-3476-9488-5bc6cc1ee7ac/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This fragment of silk was once part of a cope. This type of sleeveless hooded vestment was worn by priests at certain Christian church ceremonies. \n\nThe rows of decorative motifs here include one showing a cheetah attacking a wild ass. The other shows an ox being mauled by a lion-like beast with a fantastic tail. Such depictions of animal combat are a common feature of the art created in Iran under Safavid rule (1501-1732). They appear in a variety of media, from manuscript painting to textiles like this silk."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/e3e6223c-62ad-3476-9488-5bc6cc1ee7ac> .

